Close the access(2)/faccessat(2) write-probe existence oracle.

  access(p, W_OK)

is the cheapest probe this directory closes: one syscall, no control path, no
privilege, and it answers exactly as a stock VISIBLE file does.

  hidden injected path   -> EROFS
  genuinely absent path  -> ENOENT
  stock visible file     -> EROFS

WHY THE ENGINE CANNOT ANSWER IT -- A FOURTH ORDERING

The three mechanisms this directory already documents do not explain it:

  1. the syscall never reaches a file-level op (O_PATH, *xattr, ENOTDIR, link)
  2. mnt_want_write() answers first (truncate, utimensat, chmod, chown)
  3. existence wins over the write check (filename_create -> mkdirat/mknodat)

This is inside inode_permission() itself. sb_permission() runs FIRST and short
-circuits the whole call on a read-only superblock:

	int inode_permission(struct mnt_idmap *idmap, struct inode *inode, int mask)
	{
		retval = sb_permission(inode->i_sb, inode, mask);
		if (retval)
			return retval;			/* -EROFS */
		...
		retval = do_inode_permission(idmap, inode, mask);   /* the engine */

so EVERY MAY_WRITE query on a ROM partition is answered before the hijacked
->permission is dispatched to. Verified against v6.12 fs/namei.c.

The same short-circuit is why open(p, O_WRONLY) leaks; that half is closed in
do_open() by ghost_open.patch. This file closes the faccessat(2) half.

WHY WRITE INTENT ONLY

R_OK, X_OK and F_OK carry no MAY_WRITE, so sb_permission() returns 0 and
do_inode_permission() reaches the engine, which already answers -ENOENT for
them. Evaluating the predicate for those would put a d_path() render on one of
the hottest syscalls an app makes and close nothing. MAY_WRITE is 2 and so is
W_OK -- the same coincidence the inode_permission() call two lines below relies
on, and the `(mode & MAY_EXEC)` test immediately after the guard relies on too.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

Immediately after user_path_at() returns success, before the noexec test and
before inode_permission(). AFTER the lookup, so the parent's search permission
has already been enforced; BEFORE anything that looks at the file itself. See
the header of ghost_o_path.patch for why that is the opposite placement rule
from common/_hook/.

The error path is the function's own `goto out_path_release`, which path_put()s
the path once and reverts the AT_EACCESS credential override on the way out.

APPLIES TO

The region from `retry:` through `inode = d_backing_inode(path.dentry);` is
byte-identical on all five fleet kernel versions, even though the lines around
it are not: 5.10 calls inode_permission() with no idmap argument, 5.15/6.1 pass
a `struct user_namespace *`, 6.6/6.12 pass a `struct mnt_idmap *`, and 6.6+
spell the credential-override test access_need_override_creds(). None of that
is inside the hunk, so ONE file covers them.

Dry-run at -F0 against the pinned OnePlus trees for 5.10, 5.15, 6.1, 6.6 and
6.12: applies to all five, offsets only. Generated by editing the real file and
running `diff -up`, never by hand.

